{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"df36d71c-fe63-48a0-a426-4bf2921210e3","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I have successfully built and verified `lab-11-network-latency`.\n\nHere's a summary of what was accomplished:\n- Examined the VM environment and L05d (Active Networks) notes to extract the core learning objectives.\n- Created an isolation-safe shell script (`latency_test.sh`) using Linux network namespaces (`ns1`/`ns2`) and `veth` interfaces to cleanly measure ping/iperf3 throughput, before artificially degrading it with `tc netem`.\n- Established a `capsule.proto` schema to build `marshal_test.py`, calculating median serialization latency across C `struct.pack`, standard Python `json`, and Google `protobuf`.\n- Modeled the ANTS platform's demand-loaded execution via `capsule_sim.py`, actively walking capsules between instances to verify MD5 logic hash caching rules.\n- Drafted a CI-compliant `Makefile` allowing for automated VM testing (`make test`) and runtime execution (`make run`).\n- Wrote out a comprehensive `README.md` reflecting the actual captured latency numbers from `expected-output.txt`, along with experiments to run and conceptual checks for the concepts discussed.\n- Pushed everything locally with the conventional git commit.\n- Completed and exported the job report to `~/github/career-ops/library/01-CS-Foundations/Operating-Systems/CS6210-AOS/crew/runner/reports/lab-11-network-latency.md`.\n","duration_seconds":202.379301,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":158379,"output_tokens":14488,"thinking_tokens":4271,"cache_read_tokens":1516827,"total_tokens":172867}}}
